mott6:es-0046 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0046" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p495/b2" ;
    ev:boundingBox "320.98,70.93,563.92,358.98" ;
    ev:extractedText "A spline can be described as a series of axial keys machined into a shaft, with corresponding grooves machined into the bore of the mating part (gear, sheave, sprocket, and so on; see Figure 11–6). The splines perform the same function as a key in transmitting torque from the shaft to the mating element. The advantages of splines over keys are many. Because usually four or more splines are used, as compared with one or two keys, a more uniform transfer of the torque and a …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n11 ;
    ev:inSection mott6:sec_n11-5 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument mott6:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage mott6:page_idx495 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

mott6:es-0060 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "mott6-es-0060" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "mott6/p498/b23" ;
    ev:boundingBox "308.98,344.21,551.89,464.25" ;
    ev:extractedText "The following discussion will acquaint you with some of the ways in which power-transmitting elements can be attached to shafts without keys or splines. In most cases the designs have not been standardized, and analysis of individual cases, considering the forces exerted on the elements and the manner of loading of the fastening means, is necessary. In several of the designs, the analysis of shear and bearing will follow a procedure similar to that shown for keys. If a …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter mott6:ch_n11 ;
