shigley10:es-0056 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "shigley10-es-0056" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "shigley10/p588/b7" ;
    ev:boundingBox "196.49,457.35,547.0,540.51" ;
    ev:extractedText "A catalog load rating is defined as the radial load that causes 10 percent of a group of bearings to fail at the bearing manufacturer’s rating life. We shall denote the catalog load rating as C10. The catalog load rating is often referred to as a Basic Dynamic Load Rating, or sometimes just Basic Load Rating, if the manufacturer’s rating life is 106 revolutions. The radial load that would be necessary to cause failure at such a low life would be unrealistically high. …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter shigley10:ch_n11 ;
    ev:inSection shigley10:sec_n11-3 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument shigley10:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage shigley10:page_idx588 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

shigley10:es-0057 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "shigley10-es-0057" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "shigley10/p587/b1" ;
    ev:boundingBox "238.5,98.35,589.05,265.51" ;
    ev:extractedText "The spherical-roller thrust bearing (Fig. 11–3b) is useful where heavy loads and misalignment occur. The spherical elements have the advantage of increasing their contact area as the load is increased. Needle bearings (Fig. 11–3d) are very useful where radial space is limited. They have a high load capacity when separators are used, but may be obtained without separators. They are furnished both with and without races. Tapered roller bearings (Fig. 11–3e, f ) combine the …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
