    ev:spanOfDocument shigley10:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage shigley10:page_idx379 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

shigley10:es-0021 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "shigley10-es-0021" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "shigley10/p402/b37" ;
    ev:boundingBox "196.5,610.82,547.01,697.4" ;
    ev:extractedText "Setscrews Unlike bolts and cap screws, which depend on tension to develop a clamping force, the setscrew depends on compression to develop the clamping force. The resistance to axial motion of the collar or hub relative to the shaft is called holding power. This holding power, which is really a force resistance, is due to frictional resistance of the contacting portions of the collar and shaft as well as any slight penetration of the setscrew into the shaft."@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter shigley10:ch_n7 ;
    ev:inSection shigley10:sec_n7-7 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument shigley10:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage shigley10:page_idx402 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

shigley10:es-0023 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "shigley10-es-0023" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "shigley10/p404/b1" ;
    ev:boundingBox "196.5,98.35,547.01,145.51" ;
    ev:extractedText "resisting thrust, and the tangential holding power, for resisting torsion. Typical factors of safety are 1.5 to 2.0 for static loads and 4 to 8 for various dynamic loads. Setscrews should have a length of about half of the shaft diameter. Note that this practice also provides a rough rule for the radial thickness of a hub or collar."@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter shigley10:ch_n7 ;
    ev:inSection shigley10:sec_n7-7 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument shigley10:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage shigley10:page_idx404 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

shigley10:es-0030 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "shigley10-es-0030" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "shigley10/p404/b2" ;
    ev:boundingBox "196.5,154.93,547.0,265.51" ;
    ev:extractedText "Keys and Pins Keys and pins are used on shafts to secure rotating elements, such as gears, pulleys, or other wheels. Keys are used to enable the transmission of torque from the shaft to the shaft-supported element. Pins are used for axial positioning and for the transfer of torque or thrust or both. Figure 7–16 shows a variety of keys and pins. Pins are useful when the principal loading is shear and when both torsion and thrust are present. Taper pins are sized according to …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter shigley10:ch_n7 ;
    ev:inSection shigley10:sec_n7-7 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument shigley10:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage shigley10:page_idx404 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

shigley10:es-0035 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "shigley10-es-0035" ;
    mdcore:analystNote "In tension with Mott's design-procedure recommendation of N=3 for accidental overloads and shock. Preserved as a differing design intent."@en ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "shigley10/p405/b2" ;
    ev:boundingBox "238.5,455.95,589.03,647.11" ;
    ev:extractedText "The square key, shown in Fig. 7–16a, is also available in rectangular sizes. Standard sizes of these, together with the range of applicable shaft diameters, are listed in Table 7–6. The shaft diameter determines standard sizes for width, height, and key depth. The designer chooses an appropriate key length to carry the torsional load. Failure of the key can be by direct shear, or by bearing stress. Example 7–6 demonstrates the process to size the length of a key. The maximum …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter shigley10:ch_n7 ;
    ev:inSection shigley10:sec_n7-7 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument shigley10:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage shigley10:page_idx405 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

shigley10:es-0050 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "shigley10-es-0050" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "shigley10/p588/b1" ;
    ev:boundingBox "196.5,98.35,547.01,277.51" ;
    ev:extractedText "The rating life is a term sanctioned by the ABMA and used by most manufacturers. The rating life of a group of nominally identical ball or roller bearings is defined as the number of revolutions (or hours at a constant speed) that 90 percent of a group of bearings will achieve or exceed before the failure criterion develops. The terms minimum life, L10 life, and B10 life are also used as synonyms for rating life. The rating life is the 10th percentile location of the bearing …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter shigley10:ch_n11 ;
    ev:inSection shigley10:sec_n11-3 ;
    ev:mathFontCharRatio 0.0 ;
    ev:spanOfDocument shigley10:document ;
    ev:spanOnPage shigley10:page_idx588 ;
    ev:textIntegrity "reliable" .

shigley10:es-0051 a ev:EvidenceSpan ;
    rdfs:label "shigley10-es-0051" ;
    ev:blockIdentifier "shigley10/p588/b1" ;
    ev:boundingBox "196.5,98.35,547.01,277.51" ;
    ev:extractedText "The rating life is a term sanctioned by the ABMA and used by most manufacturers. The rating life of a group of nominally identical ball or roller bearings is defined as the number of revolutions (or hours at a constant speed) that 90 percent of a group of bearings will achieve or exceed before the failure criterion develops. The terms minimum life, L10 life, and B10 life are also used as synonyms for rating life. The rating life is the 10th percentile location of the bearing …"@en ;
    ev:extractionConfidence 0.95 ;
    ev:extractionMethod "pymupdf-textdict+anchor-verified" ;
    ev:inChapter shigley10:ch_n11 ;
